# Accessibility (mandatory)

Every interactive or meaningful element must work with screen readers and keyboard navigation.

## Required practices

- Use semantic HTML first (`button`, `a`, `nav`, `main`, headings in order).
- Icon-only controls: `aria-label` (or visible text + `aria-hidden` on the icon). Don't rely on `title` alone.
- Images: meaningful `alt`; decorative images use `alt=""` and `aria-hidden="true"` if needed.
- Modals/dialogs: `role="dialog"`, `aria-modal="true"`, `aria-labelledby` / `aria-describedby`, focus trap, Escape to close, restore focus on close. Prefer Angular CDK where appropriate.
- Dynamic state: `aria-expanded`, `aria-pressed`, `aria-live` for announcements.
- Custom controls: appropriate `role`, keyboard support, visible focus styles.
- Use existing `.sr_only` for visually hidden but screen-reader-visible text.

## Avoid

- Clickable `div`/`span` without role, keyboard support, and accessible name.
- Removing focus outlines without an accessible alternative.
- `tabindex` > 0.

## Example (icon button)

BAD: `<button class="webfont webfont-info" title="Informations"></button>`

GOOD:

```html
<button
  type="button"
  class="webfont webfont-info"
  aria-label="Informations de la page"
  [attr.aria-expanded]="isDisplayed"
></button>
```
